---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import Scroll from "../components/Scroll.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import Corrections from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Corrections.astro";
import { OWN, INHERITED, nOwn, CAUSES, causeTally } from "../lib/errors.js";

const tally = causeTally();
const top = tally[0];

/* Each error was a three-row table of its own, thirty-two of them. The shared
   page sets the retraction above the truth, which is the same pair every
   archive in the estate keeps, and the lesson stays under each one because it
   is the reason this page exists. */
const esc = (t) => String(t ?? "").replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;");
const own = OWN.map((e) => ({
  when: e.when,
  was: esc(e.what),
  now: esc(e.truth),
  how: esc(e.why),
  why: `<strong>The lesson.</strong> ${esc(e.lesson)}`,
  where: e.href,
  whereLabel: "Where it was corrected \u2192",
}));
const ord = (n) => {
  const t = n % 100, o = ["th", "st", "nd", "rd"][t > 10 && t < 14 ? 0 : n % 10] || "th";
  return n + o;
};


---
<Base
  title="What This Archive Got Wrong"
  eyebrow="Its own errors, gathered"
  dek={`${nOwn} mistakes this archive made and then corrected in public, with what caused each one. An archive that asks to be trusted about other people's errors has to be legible about its own.`}>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Every page here carries a confidence label, and the method only means anything if
    it is applied to the archive itself. These are errors <strong>this site published</strong> — not
    inherited from the family tree, but made here — together with what caused each one and what it
    should teach the next person.</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>None of these was quietly edited away.</strong> Each correction still stands on the page
      where the error was made, which is why the links below go to the page rather than to an
      apology. Two were caught within hours. One stood for a day — and it was the worst kind, a
      confident correction aimed at the family about their own family.
    </Note>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <Corrections rows={own} u={u} />
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the errors that came with the tree</h2>
    <p class="dek">These are a different thing, and worth keeping separate: claims inherited from the
    exported family tree and overturned here. They are somebody else's mistakes, made in good faith,
    and every one of them is still shown on the page that corrects it rather than deleted.</p>
    <Scroll>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The tree said</th><th>The record says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {INHERITED.map(([a, b, href]) => (
            <tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b} <a href={u(href)}>→</a></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>
  </section>

  <Blk label={`Read across all ${nOwn}`} title="The shared cause, counted">
    <p class="lede">An archive that corrects itself silently is asking to be trusted on exactly the
    point it has just proved it can be wrong about. But the pattern across these {nOwn} is worth
    more than any one of them, so on 20 September 2026 every error above was re-read and assigned a
    cause from its own text.</p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">They are not {tally.length} separate faults. They are
    {tally.length} ways of making a single one: <strong>publishing a statement about this archive's
    own tools, notes or memory as though it were a statement about the world.</strong></p>

    <Scroll>
      <table style="margin-top:1.3rem">
        <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th class="m">How many</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{tally.map(([k, n]) => (
          <tr style={k === top[0] ? "background:var(--tint)" : ""}>
            <td><strong>{CAUSES[k][0]}</strong></td>
            <td class="m">{n}</td>
            <td class="small">{CAUSES[k][1]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
      </table>
    </Scroll>

    <Note style="border-left-width:3px;margin-top:1.4rem">
      <strong>The largest group is {top[1]} of {nOwn}, and it is the one this archive built a gate
      for.</strong> A null is only a fact about a parish if the search could have found the thing it
      did not find. That is why every null on <a href={u("/searched/")}>/searched/</a> must name a
      control, why the build refuses one that does not, and why three separate checks written this
      month were thrown away for being unable to fail.
    </Note>

    <Note style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>This section replaced a sentence that had stopped being true.</strong> It used to read
      “five of the first seven were the same failure: a number or a claim taken from a secondary
      source”. That was accurate when there were seven. At {nOwn} it is the smallest group of the
      {tally.length}, and the claim had quietly become a typed number of exactly the kind it was
      describing. The tallies above are counted from the data on every build and cannot drift again.
    </Note>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">If you find a {ord(nOwn + 1)}, it belongs here.
    <a href={u("/method/")}>How claims are labelled →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What is still open →</a></p>
  </Blk>

</Base>
